  "subject": "Claim-seat application for canonical-implementor (currently status:informal, NO incumbent record — this would be the first recorded incumbent, same informal→staffed shape as chief-strategist on 2026-10-31). Fit: GOOD, and better than it looks for three reasons — the seat's bot identity canonical-implementor@catdef.org is ALREADY RATIFIED by CA-013 Call 3 (no provisional wording needed, unlike the strategist seat's CA-001/007/008 path); this seat does NOT need an MCP key per the explicit 2026-05-26-1500 brief, so the credential gap that renders the strategist's queue-triage half inert does not gate this seat; and CA-013 Call 2's deploy pipeline is live and working (CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN set 2026-05-26T16:38:35Z, last push-triggered run succeeded in 24s), closing the Director-action item from the 2026-05-26-1600 ship memo. TWO FINDINGS from reading the worker source during fit evaluation: (1) catdef.org/mcp/issue-key is ADVERTISED in src/mcp/server.ts:53 but has NO route handler — the self-serve standard-key path an external agent needs to file feedback at all was never built; strategist's §3.1 listed this 'not verified', now verified ABSENT, bears directly on #41 and plugin #1; (2) GET /mcp 404 confirmed at source — /mcp is POST-only with no GET handler or fallthrough pointer, so the observed 404 is the source as written, not misconfiguration. Also confirmed: no ZIP/inflate logic anywhere in catdef.org/src/, so CA-017/#45 is unhandled on the PROXY side as well as the client side. FOUR PRECONDITIONS disclosed, all access-shaped not capability-shaped: catdef.org is outside this session's working directories (reads verified, writes will prompt per-call; the 2026-05-26 incumbent had it configured, this session does not); a push to catdef.org main IS a production deploy with no staging step, so push/deploy is treated as requiring explicit per-act Director authorization and NOT a standing seat privilege; local git identity is set to the WRONG seat (catdef-maintainer) and must be changed before any commit; GitHub issue attribution stays blocked on the §2.1 problem though commit attribution does not, since commit authorship can carry the bot identity while the push uses the Director's token per the e641be2/5d3e453 precedent. Awaiting authorization — no seat recorded, no charter edit, nothing built, nothing pushed.",

  "sent": "2026-11-01T09:32:48-05:00",
  "action_required": true,

  "body": "Application to occupy canonical-implementor. **Oriented first** via /og-orient this session against the charter, both CLAUDE.md levels, CONTRIBUTING.md, the CA-001…CA-018 chain, all 50 memo artifacts, and proposals/ — this card rests on that read rather than honouring it on the honour system. **Fit: good.** The position scopes this seat to the render.catdef.org L1 reference renderer, the /fetch SSRF-guarded proxy, catdef-family namespaced-type rendering, and MCP-tool integration, operating from the catdef.org repo. That is implementation work — code, conformance suite, git/gh, curl verification against deployed behavior — and it matches what this session can do. Rather than assume the surface, I read it: see the two findings below. **Three things make the fit stronger than the position description alone suggests.** (1) The bot identity `canonical-implementor <canonical-implementor@catdef.org>` is already RATIFIED by CA-013 Call 3 — used provisionally on catdef-plugin e641be2 and catdef.org 5d3e453, then ratified with 'no provisional wording, matching the ratified posture'. This seat needs no identity ratification to proceed, unlike the strategist seat's CA-001/007/008 → CA-009 path. (2) This seat does not need an MCP key: the 2026-05-26-1500 pickup brief states outright that 'the canonical-implementor seat for v0.2+ work doesn't need a seat key — implementer work is local-repo + git-push + wrangler-deploy, not MCP-tool-call.' The elevated-key gap that currently makes the strategist seat's queue-triage half inert does not gate this one. (3) The deploy pipeline works: CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN is set on catdef/catdef.org (2026-05-26T16:38:35Z) and the latest push-triggered run succeeded in 24s, which closes the 'Director action required' item left open by the 2026-05-26-1600 ship memo. **Prior occupancy:** operated 2026-05-25 evening → 2026-05-26 by claude-opus-4-7-1m (shipped catdef-plugin v1.4.0, catdef.org/mcp v0.1 and v0.1.1, the CA-016 renderer move). Those sessions recorded only drafted_by_session prose, predating the 2026-08-25 binding convention, so there is NO prior binding on this address to supersede — the occupancies are noted as dated facts, not as bindings. **Two findings from the fit read, filed because they are this seat's to own and NOT as a start of work.** Finding 1: catdef.org/mcp/issue-key does not exist. src/mcp/server.ts:53 tells callers 'Standard keys (cdfk_<random>) are self-serve; ask the Director or check catdef.org/mcp/issue-key' and that string is the ONLY occurrence in the source. The complete route table is GET /, GET /render, GET /fetch, GET /spec, POST /feedback, GET /feedback, POST /mcp, POST /mcp/admin/refresh-counter — no issue-key handler. The server advertises a self-serve key path that was never built, which means the route to a standard key (what an external agent needs to file feedback at all) is absent. The strategist's §3.1 listed this as 'not verified'; it is now verified absent, and it bears directly on #41's acceptance criterion and on plugin #1. Finding 2: the GET /mcp 404 is confirmed at source — /mcp is POST-only, no GET handler, no fallthrough pointer, so the observed 404 is the source behaving as written rather than a misconfiguration. Also confirmed consistent with the strategist's report: no ZIP, inflate, or decompression logic anywhere in catdef.org/src/, so CA-017 / #45 is unhandled on the PROXY side as well as the client side, matching the 502 'Upstream body is not valid UTF-8' exactly. Two patch-level items alongside: renderer/index.html carries no build number (shared project principles mandate one on every page), and renderer/README.md calls catdef.org 'the private catdef.org operations repo' while gh reports it PUBLIC — stale either way, and worth knowing which of the two is wrong. **Preconditions, disclosed rather than minimized.** (a) The seat's stated workspace is outside this session's working directories: the clone at s:/projects-personal/catdef.org is readable (verified) but not configured, so cd is reset on exit and writes will prompt per call; the 2026-05-26 incumbent recorded having it as an additional working directory and this session does not. Remedy: add it, or accept per-call prompts. (b) A push to catdef.org main IS a production deploy — CA-013 Call 2's workflow leaves no staging step between commit and catdef.org/render.catdef.org, so push and deploy are treated here as outward-facing acts requiring explicit per-act Director authorization, never a standing seat privilege; the 2026-05-26 precedent pushed and ran wrangler deploy under PO authorization given at the time, and that is not read as a grant to this session. (c) Local git identity is the WRONG seat — user.name/user.email are catdef-maintainer/catdef-maintainer@catdef.org, so any commit now would mis-attribute to a seat holding no incumbent; must be set to the ratified canonical-implementor identity before this seat commits. (d) GitHub issue attribution is unresolved but commit attribution is not: the §2.1 problem applies to issues and comments (which would land under scottconfusedgorilla) and NOT to commits, since commit authorship can carry the bot identity while the push uses the Director's token — exactly the e641be2 / 5d3e453 precedent. (e) No published roledef and no embedded roledef:Job (recommended_roles cites canonical-implementor as a bare string with no URL; org/jobs/ holds only .gitkeep), so specialization is deferred per the card. (f) This seat is directed, not self-directing — chief-strategist directs it, it reports_to director, and the strategist seat is LIVE and reachable as catdef-strategist [75e16c]; priorities come from the Director or that seat, not from this session's own reading of the backlog. **Scope accepted:** implementation and verification of the L1 renderer (catdef-spec/renderer/), the catdef.org worker (/fetch proxy, /render, MCP server surface), catdef-family namespaced-type rendering, conformance runs against own changes, status memos to the strategist seat per milestone — steady-state catdef-substrate concerns only per x.position.lifecycle. **Bounds NOT held:** no ratification, no governance decisions, no normative spec-text edits (CATDEF_SPEC.md / CATIO_SPEC.md / MCP_REFERENCE.md are the maintainer's draft and the Director's ratification), no merge, no push or deploy without per-act authorization, and the no-in-flight-amendment red line applies to this seat as to any implementer — a spec gap met during implementation gets filed as feedback, not edited into the spec. Issue and PR creation stays blocked on (d). **Queue inherited, listed and NOT started:** from the 2026-05-26-1430 closeout — feedback_audit table, mechanical catdef_validate pytest port, Durable Object for CA-NNN under concurrency, resourceTemplates in catdef_describe (CA-012 v0.2 follow-on), self-serve API-key issuance endpoint (which Finding 1 shows is advertised and absent); from the strategist's 2026-11-01 report — CATIO ZIP support in /fetch and the client loader (CA-017/#45, both sides now confirmed unhandled), the renderer build number, the GET /mcp pointer and issue-key work for #41; plus CA-016 Directive 2, still bundled for a maintainer cycle that has not run. Sequencing is the Director's and the strategist's. **Not done:** seat not taken, charter not edited, no file in catdef.org touched, nothing committed/pushed/deployed, no GitHub issue created or commented, no spec text modified, no queue item started.",
  "body_ref": "2026-11-01-0932--canonical-implementor--director--claim-seat-application.body.md",

